synth: a design-supplied script hook that runs before flatten

synth's -extra-map is applied inside the techmap step, which comes after
flatten. By then a module the design defines itself has been inlined and
there are no cells of its type left to match, so -extra-map cannot
substitute one.

That is the case whenever a design must replace one of its own modules
rather than a yosys primitive. A vendored core's clock gate is the
example that wanted it: VeeR EH1 defines its gate in beh_lib.sv and
names it through a macro that names the definition and the
instantiation together, so the module cannot be redirected from outside
the sources. Left alone it synthesizes to a transparent latch and an
AND -- 952 latch cells on that configuration -- where a real flow maps
it onto the library's integrated clock gate. The difference is not only
area: a transparent latch lets STA borrow time across it, so every
register-to-register path through a gated clock reports slack that a
flop-based path would not.

The hook is a yosys script rather than a list of techmap files because
substituting a module is rarely just techmap. Under the slang frontend
it never is: slang elaborates one module per instance and names each
after the instance path, so the design holds a hundred differently
named copies of the gate and none of type `clockhdr`. Folding them back
onto one name (chtype) has to happen in the same window, and only the
design knows which names to fold.

hierarchy is re-run afterwards so the modules the substitution orphaned
are pruned rather than carried into flatten and synthesized as dead
logic.

Not upstreamed. Retire at a bump onto an ORFS that carries a hook for
this; upstreaming it should also add the variable to variables.yaml,
which this patch deliberately leaves alone to keep the diff to the one
file whose behaviour changes.
---
diff --git a/flow/scripts/synth.tcl b/flow/scripts/synth.tcl
--- a/flow/scripts/synth.tcl
+++ b/flow/scripts/synth.tcl
@@ -66,6 +66,27 @@
 
 hierarchy -check -top $::env(DESIGN_NAME)
 
+# Design-supplied yosys scripts, run while the hierarchy is intact.
+#
+# synth's own -extra-map runs inside the techmap step, which is after
+# flatten: by then a module the design defines itself has been inlined
+# and there are no cells of its type left to match. A design that has to
+# substitute one of its own modules -- a vendored core's clock gate,
+# whose name is fixed by a macro that names the definition and the
+# instantiation together, so it cannot be redirected from outside --
+# needs the substitution to happen here instead. It is a script and not
+# a list of map files because the substitution generally needs more than
+# techmap: under slang each instance is its own module, and folding
+# those names back onto one is part of the same step.
+if { [env_var_exists_and_non_empty SYNTH_POST_HIERARCHY_SCRIPTS] } {
+  foreach synth_post_hierarchy_script $::env(SYNTH_POST_HIERARCHY_SCRIPTS) {
+    log_cmd script $synth_post_hierarchy_script
+  }
+  # Prune whatever the substitution orphaned, so flatten does not carry
+  # it in and synthesize it as dead logic.
+  log_cmd hierarchy -check -top $::env(DESIGN_NAME)
+}
+
 if { $::env(SYNTH_GUT) } {
   # /deletes all cells at the top level, which will quickly optimize away
   # everything else, including macros.
